WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.533
Policy.

2
00:00:01.833 --> 00:00:07.000
Values, buffers and critics all exist for one reason: to improve this one mapping.

3
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.442
A policy maps a state to an action.

4
00:00:10.442 --> 00:00:11.467
It is the agent.

5
00:00:11.767 --> 00:00:15.633
A deterministic policy gives one fixed answer for each state.

6
00:00:15.933 --> 00:00:20.100
A stochastic policy gives a probability distribution over actions.

7
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.333
Here is a policy for a small grid.

8
00:00:23.633 --> 00:00:26.867
Each cell simply holds an arrow: the action to take there.

9
00:00:27.167 --> 00:00:29.425
One cell keeps a little randomness.

10
00:00:29.425 --> 00:00:32.200
Exploration lives inside the policy itself.

11
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.533
Stochastic policies matter more than they first appear.

12
00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:41.267
Deterministic ones give one action and have no built-in exploration.

13
00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:43.567
Stochastic ones explore naturally.

14
00:00:43.867 --> 00:00:48.300
And they are differentiable, which policy-gradient methods like PPO need.

15
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:54.200
An LLM generating tokens is exactly this: a distribution over next actions.

16
00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.200
Policies are easy to compare.

17
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:01.200
A random policy averages about minus zero point four.

18
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:07.167
A greedy policy, picking the best known action, averages about plus zero point nine four.

19
00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:10.300
Here are those two policies in code.

20
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:12.667
The random policy picks any action.

21
00:01:12.967 --> 00:01:17.300
The greedy policy picks the action with the highest value in the Q-table.

22
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.633
Averaged over episodes, that gap is the whole point.

23
00:01:21.833 --> 00:01:24.233
Ask of any RL system: what ships?

24
00:01:24.533 --> 00:01:28.600
The training rig stays home: buffer, optimizer, reward function.

25
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:33.667
Only the policy goes up: a sixteen row table, or a twenty kilobyte network.

26
00:01:33.967 --> 00:01:35.075
Same as an LLM.

27
00:01:35.075 --> 00:01:37.733
The served model is a frozen policy.

28
00:01:38.933 --> 00:01:39.733
Let's recap.

29
00:01:39.733 --> 00:01:41.867
The policy maps state to action.

30
00:01:41.867 --> 00:01:42.933
It is the agent.

31
00:01:43.233 --> 00:01:45.700
It can be deterministic or stochastic.

32
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.100
Stochastic policies explore and are differentiable.

33
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.149
And only the policy ships.

34
00:01:51.149 --> 00:01:53.033
Next up: the value function.
